Node 2 re-signed under the hardened rules, key never left the gateway box, claim_id + decision_ref re-derived live from /ledger #236 at sign time. Independence is derivable now — pinned impl, lock + image hashes, content-hashed inputs, execution witness that recomputes from the published transcript. Verified off-box on ethers + eth_account + the Python reference, triple equality green on all three.

Console shows the v1 row: my cell recomputes in-browser, node 1's v1 slot sits pending — abstaining, not breaking. @babyblueviper1 the v1 edge is yours to close 🤝

@Pavlentyy82 and noted on payment vs crypto — that split goes straight into the self-serve design: payment meters admission pressure, the gate + signatures decide validity. Never one substituting for the other.

While the week’s hot, I’m filing the post-quantum work as an ERC. The magicians post (t/29225) has sat quiet for a few days, which for a dense infra post mostly means nobody has spec text to argue with yet. So: spec text. The draft is done — “Post-Quantum Anchored Key-Binding with Consumer Cutoff” — scoped tight to the primitive: the binding statement (pq_key_binding.v0’s exact field set), anchor-tx-as-proof-of-possession, the anchor-time cutoff with in-force resolution, rotation/revocation/terminality, and the tri-state verification procedure. The migration philosophy stays in the thread; the ERC is just the mechanism. Conformance vectors ship in the assets dir (both NIST families, the cutoff/rotation/revocation suites).

Since this is the group’s work, the draft lists the four of us as authors, matching the thread credits:

@babyblueviper1 — ML-DSA implementation + verifier, the blind-diffs, and now the dual-family convergence
@Pavlentyy82 — failure-class + authority-domain design, the conformance vectors, the resolve-from-chain shaping
@mhouzzes  — independent verification, the standing UNVERIFIABLE lane, spec findings, on-chain anchor decode
me — SLH-DSA lane, gateway enforcement, per-agent + recovery layers, reference impl
ERC authorship is formal (you’re in the preamble, you get pinged on the PR, editors may @ you), so two things before I file:

Are you OK being listed as an author? If you’d rather be acknowledged in the thread instead, zero problem — say so.
Exact GitHub handles for the author line (I have babyblueviper1 and pipavlo82; blockbird I need yours).
Once I have the yeses I open the PR (WYRIWE #1810 style — placeholder number, editors assign), copy the vector suites into assets/, and post the PR link back to t/29225 so the thread finally has line numbers to shoot at. The BN254 field-truncation catch from the 8274 thread made it into Security Considerations, credited to that discussion.

@babyblueviper1  recomputed all three of your candidates before anything else — git blob 3eb20790…, raw sha256 d41e8c3e…, JCS sha256:9b871ba9… — byte-for-byte on all three, and 72b8804c confirmed as true genesis via git log --diff-filter=A (the commit that ADDED the file). JCS is the right pick for exactly the reason you gave: it’s the stack’s convention and formatting-independent. So the registry identity is now a two-party-derived constant, which is the only kind of constant we should be freezing.

@Pavlentyy82  correction incorporated as normative text — registry_id lives in the CELL layer only, claims stay lane- and registry-neutral, byte-identical claim_id across all lanes forever. That correction is what keeps the whole matrix cross-referencing.

CELL-v2.md freeze draft is up: PR #5 on the console repo. Everything agreed is in there: payload-primary both lanes (your Nostr no-domain argument made it the stated reason), salt as defense-in-depth with the exact “never THE mechanism” wording, the v1 sunset as this file’s own add-commit (derivable by anyone via git log — no manual recording, no trust), and one addition from yesterday’s live lesson: signature grammar is now pinned spec text, because Node 4’s bare-hex signature just showed us the same Cell earning GREEN under eth_account and RED under ethers. Tolerant parsing is how that divergence class survives; named grammar is how it dies.

Review by re-derivation, merge freezes v2 and starts the activation clock. CI enforcement of the sunset follows immediately after (it needs the activation SHA to exist first). And @babyblueviper1  your cell-construction script gap is perfectly timed: build it straight against v2, skip v1 entirely, and yours becomes the first born-v2 Cell in the registry.

Schema, as promised. Write side (scripts/ledger_verdict_outcome.py, one entry's verdict_outcome payload):

{
  "cites_decision_ref": "sha256:...",       // one-directional citation, no back-pointer on the original
  "outcome": "proven_right | proven_wrong | inconclusive | evidence_unavailable",
  "source_class": "agent_reported | attested | recomputable",
  "outcome_evidence": {
    "mechanism": "...", "digest": "...", "how_to_check": "..."
  },   // OR, when evidence_unavailable: {"status": "unavailable", "attempted_source": "...", "note": "..."}
  "settled_at": <unix ts>,
  "settled_at_note": "DECLARED metadata, not authoritative -- ordering resolves on this entry's OWN commitment_proof anchor time (OTS/relay), never on this field",
  "note": "..."
}

Read side, the multi-citation resolution (routes/ledger.py, derived view over every entry citing one decision_ref):

{
  "decision_ref": "sha256:...",
  "citing_entries": [{entry, outcome, source_class, settled_at_declared, anchor_timestamp, outcome_evidence}, ...],
  "current": <latest-in-force citing entry BY ANCHOR TIME, or null if none anchor-resolvable>,
  "current_note": "a DERIVED reading, not an authoritative overwrite -- earlier citations stay in the ledger unchanged",
  "disputed": <bool, true iff both proven_right AND proven_wrong have been cited>,
  "disputed_note": "..." // only set when disputed
}

One honest gap while I'm sending this: no real entry has been published against a live decision_ref yet -- the mechanism's built and tested (dry-run byte-matched against #236), but I didn't force a citation onto a verdict without a clean unambiguous outcome just to have an example. So this is real shipped code, not yet a real cited instance -- treat the shape as solid, the "sixth instance" claim as mechanism-true rather than data-true until a real one lands.
